// Lesson 6.4 — Double Jump: Moving Jumps to StatManager
// Level 3 (Outline Only, Short Lesson ~15 min for Friday Nov 20):
// Move `jumpsRemaining` and `maxJumps` from PlayerController onto StatManager
// (`public int jumpsRemaining; public int maxJumps = 1;` — or `2` / pickup upgrade)
// so jump capacity lives alongside health & coins and survives scene switches / Die().
// Remaining class time: finishing cave level & getting ready for the boss fight!
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big: 'Move the jump counter onto StatManager so multi-jump upgrades persist everywhere and unlock double jumping.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Move jumpsRemaining and maxJumps into StatManager',
      doc: '5.3 (`StatManager` singleton + `public` fields vs private fields with methods)',
      href: '5.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Point PlayerController at manager.jumpsRemaining and manager.maxJumps',
      doc: '6.2 (`Start()`, `Update()`, and `OnCollisionEnter2D()` inside `PlayerController.cs`)',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'In `6.2` we put `jumpsRemaining` and `maxJumps` on `PlayerController`, which resets every time a scene loads. Moving them into `StatManager` works identically for health and coins: `StatManager` (`DontDestroyOnLoad`) remembers whether you unlocked a double jump (`maxJumps = 2`) across every door and respawn.',
  turns: [
    'Look at `StatManager.cs`: `health`, `maxHealth`, and `coins` have NO `public` keyword, so why did `PlayerController` need `manager.ChangeHealth()` and `manager.ChangeCoins()` — and what happens if you move `jumpsRemaining` and `maxJumps` onto `StatManager` without marking them `public`?',
    'Once `jumpsRemaining` and `maxJumps` live on `StatManager`, which three places in `PlayerController.cs` need `manager.` added in front of them (and where do you still reset `manager.jumpsRemaining = manager.maxJumps`)?',
  ],
};

const body = `
${renderAskBanner('how do I make a double jump?', 3)}

<p class="lead">
    <strong>Short lesson (~15 min) + Cave Level / Boss Prep Studio.</strong> In
    <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> you built <code>jumpsRemaining</code> and <code>maxJumps = 1</code>
    inside <code>PlayerController</code>. Today we move both integers into <code>StatManager</code>
    so a double jump (<code>maxJumps = 2</code>) persists across scenes and respawns — then spend
    the rest of Friday finishing your cave before Thanksgiving Break.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderDecompose('6.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code: Move Jump State to StatManager (~10 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Script</th><th>What moves / changes</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>StatManager.cs</code></td>
            <td>Add <code>public int jumpsRemaining;</code> and <code>public int maxJumps = 1;</code> (or set <code>maxJumps = 2</code> when testing / unlocking boots)</td>
            <td>C# fields default to <code>private</code>; adding <code>public</code> (or public helper methods) lets <code>PlayerController</code> read and update jump count across scripts, while <code>DontDestroyOnLoad()</code> keeps unlocks alive.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>PlayerController.cs</code></td>
            <td>Remove local <code>jumpsRemaining</code> / <code>maxJumps</code>; update <code>Start()</code>, <code>Update()</code>, and <code>OnCollisionEnter2D()</code> to use <code>manager.jumpsRemaining</code> and <code>manager.maxJumps</code></td>
            <td>Keeps one source of truth instead of two competing copies of the counter.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Optional Upgrade Pickup</td>
            <td>In <code>OnTriggerEnter2D()</code>, check a tag (e.g. <code>"Boots"</code>) to run <code>manager.maxJumps = 2; manager.jumpsRemaining = manager.maxJumps; Destroy(other.gameObject);</code></td>
            <td>Reuses the exact coin-pickup pattern from <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> to unlock a permanent double jump.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Check your entry scene when testing:</strong> if you press Play inside <code>Platformer</code> directly, make sure there is a <code>StatManager</code> in the scene (or launch from <code>Start (index 0)</code>) so <code>manager = FindAnyObjectByType&lt;StatManager&gt;();</code> never returns <code>null</code>.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Ownership &amp; Multi-Jump Data</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> you had to change 0 jump lines inside <code>Update()</code> or <code>OnCollisionEnter2D()</code> to turn single jump into double jump besides changing which object owns <code>maxJumps</code>. Why does storing a count instead of a <code>bool canJump</code> scale to 2, 3, or 5 jumps for free?</li>
            <li>What happens in C# if you move <code>jumpsRemaining</code> onto <code>StatManager</code> as <code>int jumpsRemaining;</code> without typing <code>public</code>?</li>
            <li>When the player dies and <code>StatManager.Die()</code> reloads the current scene, does a Boots pickup (<code>manager.maxJumps = 2</code>) stay unlocked or disappear? Contrast that with how coins behave in <code>Die()</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor + Studio: Finish Cave &amp; Prep for Boss Week (~55 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~55 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>Test <code>manager.maxJumps = 2</code> directly on <code>StatManager</code> (or via a <code>"Boots"</code> pickup in your cave), verify two jumps work mid-air, and confirm touching <code>"Ground"</code> refills both.</li>
        <li>Audit every high platform or gap in <code>Platformer.unity</code>: decide which jumps require single-jump + moving platforms/ladders and which secret areas reward a double jump.</li>
        <li>Playtest the whole progression from <code>Overworld</code> → <code>Platformer</code> → end-of-cave boss doorway, and commit + push before Thanksgiving Break.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain why moving <code>jumpsRemaining</code> and <code>maxJumps</code> onto <code>StatManager</code> keeps double jump state alive across scenes</li>
        <li>Use <code>public</code> fields (or public methods) cleanly so <code>PlayerController</code> reads and resets <code>manager.jumpsRemaining</code></li>
        <li>Verify double jump resets on <code>"Ground"</code> collision and never allows a third air jump</li>
        <li>Leave your cave playable and ready for independent Boss Fight building after Thanksgiving</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

